#{[17592186411873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x-2y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4bd89e0-666c-5b6d-a3d4-7d170f1093d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x-2y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -2=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-2 \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 1+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 1+y & =2 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -1+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -1+y & =2 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,0),\\,(-1,3,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,0)$ | $(-1,3,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,3,0),$ and its  minimum value is $-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a66f9263c9009f634668e826f0a84320103589b9c270360d7b2465c71e74592.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}2xz\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea08d184-a351-56ae-9a07-8c580e0db18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/49/49a3333a52a6b7d7451095dbc46ab1009df491c92726436929a6f93cff38db60.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-2x\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dfa0a80bc323401f050d1302dc42c92558d8b566d9b0f985e955d34eb5cc15bd.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-2x\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[x{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=3-2x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3-2x)^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}){[y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18x-24{x}^{2}+8{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(18x-24{x}^{2}+8{x}^{3})\\,dx & ={[9{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}+2{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[9-8+2] \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1045 "Weighted Least-Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186412663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/